<Guide slug="ramadan-travel" photo="marrakech">
  <p>
    Ramadan is the Islamic month of fasting, when observant Muslims don’t eat or drink from dawn to sunset. It follows the lunar
    calendar, so it moves about 11 days earlier each year. <strong>Ramadan 2027 is expected to begin on or around 8 February
    and end around 9 March</strong>, followed by the Eid al-Fitr holiday; the exact dates depend on the sighting of the new
    moon. In 2028 it is expected from about 28 January to 26 February.
  </p>
  <p>
    Travelling during Ramadan is easy and often rewarding: sights stay open, visitors aren’t expected to fast, and the evenings
    are among the most atmospheric of the year. A little planning and respect go a long way.
  </p>

  <h2>What changes for visitors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Opening hours:</b> museums, sites and government offices often keep shorter hours; shops may close in the afternoon and reopen late into the night.</li>
    <li><b>Food and drink:</b> many local restaurants close during the day, while hotels and tourist restaurants stay open. In stricter places, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in daylight.</li>
    <li><b>Iftar:</b> at sunset the fast is broken with dates and soup, and streets go suddenly quiet — then lively until late. Joining an iftar is a highlight of any Ramadan trip.</li>
    <li><b>Alcohol:</b> sales may be restricted or suspended outside hotels in some countries.</li>
    <li><b>Traffic:</b> roads are busiest in the hour before sunset as everyone rushes home.</li>
    <li><b>Eid al-Fitr:</b> the holiday at the end of Ramadan is a time of family travel — expect busy transport and higher prices for a few days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Country by country</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b><a href="/best-time-to-visit-morocco/">Morocco</a>:</b> medina life slows down by day and comes alive at night. Morocco also switches its clocks back to GMT (UTC+0) during Ramadan, so check times for flights and tours.</li>
    <li><b><a href="/best-time-to-visit-egypt/">Egypt</a>:</b> temples and museums stay open with shorter hours; Cairo glows with Ramadan lanterns (fanous) and iftar tents.</li>
    <li><b><a href="/destinations/jordan/">Jordan</a>:</b> Petra and Wadi Rum operate as normal; restaurants in Amman may be closed by day.</li>
    <li><b><a href="/destinations/dubai/">Dubai</a>, <a href="/destinations/doha/">Doha</a> and <a href="/destinations/oman/">Oman</a>:</b> malls, hotels and attractions stay open; many restaurants serve during the day, but be discreet in public places. Lavish iftar and suhoor buffets are a big part of the experience.</li>
    <li><b><a href="/best-time-to-visit-indonesia/">Indonesia</a> and <a href="/best-time-to-visit-malaysia/">Malaysia</a>:</b> Bali is mostly Hindu and largely unaffected; on Java, Lombok and in Malaysia, evening Ramadan bazaars are a food lover’s dream.</li>
    <li><b><a href="/best-time-to-visit-turkiye/">Türkiye</a>:</b> life in Istanbul carries on largely as normal, with festive iftars around Sultanahmet.</li>
  </ul>

